hat the strategy may not be  optimal for Sweden but the officials  there and the scientists are watching  the situation very carefully and trying  to react according to the Swedish system  what do you think is coming our way as a  second lockdown is that conceivable it's  certainly conceivable right now the  numbers are rising in several countries  dramatically rising and several states  for example in the United States after  lockdown have opened up and and now I  see a dramatic rise and a substantial  burden for the healthcare system so I  think at least on the local level a  second lockdown is very likely until we  have a medication or a vaccine available  to treat cope with 19 what's that going  to mean though if a second look it means  it means that well first of all commit  19 happened locally in regions so not in  a country as a whole but it's always in  regions so at the lockdown that we had  during the first wave so-called first  wave affected mostly countries so our  state levels and so hopefully by now we  have measures in place that will allow  us to if necessary have lockdowns on a  more regional more smaller level so just  a city or like a village or like a very  small region rather than a state or a  country that's what I mean when I say we  hopefully have measures in place that  will allow us to react more flexible but  does it mean opening up closing down  over and over I mean can't that go on  forever or at some stage we're going to  have to come to terms with the reality  of of living with this disease  absolutely and the reality is that the  virus is still there and we're trying to  open up and the the issue is of course  we have some of the weather's noise  people go outside and they forget that a  virus is there so wearing masks  particularly if you go inside a very  mass is very important keeping the  distance then testing tracing people  isolating people who have been tested  positive it's very important and not all  countries are ready to actually conduct  these measures that's why the ultimate  solution then is unfortunately then a  lockdown you heard it from Tobias court  epidemiologist and director of the  Institute of Public Health at the shotty  here in Berlin thank you and let's check  in with our science correspondent Derek  Williams he's been busy researching the  answers to your questions on all things  corona related can someone who has been  infected and cured of kovat 19 be  reinfected I've answered this a couple  of times over the months but it's one of  those topics that means regular updates  especially in light of a couple of  recently published studies showing that  antibody levels are dropping off in a  pretty big way in a lot of people who  had COBIT 19 within three months of  their infection especially in those who  were asymptomatic now antibody levels it  has to be said aren't the only factor to  play a role in long term immune response  but but still this isn't positive news  when it comes to our hopes that getting  kovat 19 will stop you from getting it  again for at least a year or two that  said over six months into the pandemic  there's still no clear-cut evidence of  someone being infected again after  having already been through the disease  once how likely is the transmission of  influenza on airline flights  to find out how dangerous flying could  be potentially in the in the kovat  nineteen era it's a good idea to look  back at research in planes that we've  done in the past on the spread of  viruses like influenza  now the w-h-o says transmission of  infectious pathogens happens mostly  between people seated in the same area  on the plane so the risk is highest  within those few rows but but it  concludes that the risk is otherwise no  higher than it is on on a train or a bus  or or in a theater but I tracked down a  meta-study from 2016 and one of the  interesting things it had to say was  that research on the topic really  doesn't always agree and that our data  is not really that robust so to be  truthful it looks like we still don't  know really exactly how likely pathogen  transmission is on a plane just that it  grows more likely the closer you sit to  the infected person what lessons are  being learned to prevent the next  pandemic how are they being compiled and  disseminated I think there are many  lessons to be learned or maybe re  learned from this pandemic one is that  we need to be more prepared for for when  this happens again and and sooner or  later we know it will another is that  that transparency and acting fast  especially in the early stages of a  novel pathogen outbreak is absolutely  vital unfortunately for us humans  lessons learned often don't necessarily  translate into into lessons retained  when it comes to compiling and  disseminating learned lessons it seems  to me that that the World Health  Organization would be a natural  repository but at the same time the  mistakes the w-h-o is made in the course  of this current pandemic have revealed  some some pretty deep flaws in my  opinion  the organization needs reforms that not  only give it a clearer mandate but that  also give it the means to carry that  mandate out because next time we really  have to do a better job at coordinating  an international response  [Music]
 

